  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1224 PM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA 2nd shortwave trough will move across northern NM Friday night\ninto Saturday morning, bringing another round of showers. Moisture\nadvection will ramp back up Saturday night into Sunday in advance\nof an approaching upper level trough/low, forecast to move across\nNM on Monday. This 3rd wave will be the most significant, steering \nPacific moisture and anomalously high PWATs over the region along\nwith jet dynamics and strong orographic forcing. Precipitation\nfor the Sun/Mon event will favor western NM and especially the\npeaks and higher west and southwest facing slopes. There is some \nflood potential across northwest NM Sun/Mon, but this event will \nbe a different type of event compared to last week with a strong \nlean toward orographic forcing. Flash flooding is still possible, \nbut will take longer to create problems given lower rainfall rates\nand will favor river flooding over flash flooding. This is \nespecially true given potential flows coming down the La Plata \nand Animas rivers out of southeast CO due to significant qpf for \nthe San Juan Mountains, which always perform with strong southwest\nflow events and a firehose of Pacific moisture. We'll likely be \nissuing a Flood Watch in the next 24hrs for Sun/Mon, for NW NM at \na minimum. Snow levels will start out high with this event,\nbetween 11-12Kft, but will come down to 8500-9000ft late Sunday \nnight through Monday as the 560-565dam 500mb low moves southeast \nacross the state. The northern mountains may pick up several \ninches of snow during the Sunday night through Monday period and \ncome out the backside on Tuesday with a notably white appearance. \nStrong winds are forecast both ahead of and behind a Pacific cold \nfront Monday. A warming and drying trend will begin Tuesday under\nthe influence of an upper level ridge following behind the \ndeparting upper low.",
